{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966800,"nonce":"QoHdh2kh90B8bV8JFJUUlI0gM7M=","title":"One decoration marker meant both \"re-render me when F moves\" and \"I declare F\", so a","message":"RESOLVED by cee4abc2. `__fsm_view__` carried two meanings: `@fsm([F])`\n(follow F) and `component X { fsm F { … } }` (declare F). Ownership drove\nthe mount bracket, so a component that merely READ a module-level FSM got\na machine of its own. A binding resolved before that mount ran bound the\nprocess-wide machine's field; the dispatch then wrote the mount's; the\nview kept showing the old branch while the value it named had changed.\n\n`__fsm_own__` is the declaration marker now, and only it records\nownership. Both sites in `passes/view.rs` set `owns_fsm_here` - the\ncomponent-attrs loop and the leading-marker strip - and missing the\nsecond one left the bug in place with the first fixed.\n\n**How it was found:** probing which machine each id belonged to. The\nnotification order gave it away - the id the dep list bound was notified\nBEFORE the dep list existed, and the dispatch notified two different ids.\nReasoning about the passes had produced three wrong theories first.\n\nRelated: [[gotcha-bound-prop-wrong-mount]], [[gotcha-unhandled-effect-crashes]].","files":null}]}